 is not audited and not production ready and is provided as is. Use at your own risk.\nL3: ## Overview\nL4: \nL5: DeepProve is a framework for proving inference of neural networks using cryptographic techniques based on sumchecks and logup GKR. Proving time is sublinear in the size of the model, providing order-of-magnitude speedups compared to other inference proving frameworks.\nL6: **First-class LLM support**: DeepProve proves end-to-end inference for transformer-based LLMs including **GPT-2**, **Gemma 3**, and **Llama 2**, covering all transformer layer types from embeddings through to next-token argmax. MLP and CNN inference is also supported.\nL7: The framework quantizes weights and activations into a fixed zero-centered range. The default range is `[-2^11, 2^11-1]` (12-bit symmetric quantization); the bit width can be set via the `ZKML_BIT_LEN` environment variable (typical values: 8, 10, 12, 16).\nL8: ## How DeepProve Works\nL9: \nL10: DeepProve is built on a few simple ideas. If you skim only one section of this document, this is the one.\nL11: ### 1. Certify, don't re-execute\nL12: \nL13: A naïve ZK system would re-run each token's forward pass inside a circuit. For a sequence of length `t` and a per-token circuit of size `|C|`, that gives `O(t² · |C|)` prover work — unworkable on real-world LLMs.\nL14: DeepProve instead concatenates the input tokens and the claimed output tokens into a single sequence, and runs the model on that sequence **once**. Because of the causal attention mask and the deterministic argmax at the output, the model's prediction at position `t` certifies the token at position `t+1`. We prove a single forward pass; the structure of attention does the rest.\nL15: ### 2. Pre-process the model once\nL16: \nL17: A model file in SafeTensors, GGUF, or ONNX format is parsed into an operator graph, quantized into integer-only arithmetic (post-training static quantization, calibrated on a representative dataset), and committed to with a polynomial commitment scheme. The result is a reusable proving context (`*.pk` / `*.vk`) that amortizes across every subsequent inference proof.\nL18: ### 3. Prove layer-by-layer, then chain\nL19: \nL20: For every layer in the graph we run a specialised protocol:\nL21: \nL22: - **Linear ops** (MatMul, Conv, RMSNorm, LayerNorm) — efficient sum-check protocols\nL23: - **Non-linear ops** (Softmax, GeLU, requantization) — LogUp-GKR lookup arguments\nL24: \nL25: Per-layer claims chain back-to-front in a GKR-style composition, producing a single end-to-end proof.\nL26: The polynomial commitment scheme used in the public binaries is **HyperKZG**, built on top of [arkworks](https://arkworks.rs/) and therefore generic over any pairing-friendly curve. The CPU build defaults to BN254; the GPU build (`--features cuda`) is currently BN254-only.\nL27: ## Documentation\nL28: \nL29: Deeper technical write-ups — proof protocols for each layer (embeddings, QKV, multi-head attention, positional encoding, LayerNorm, RMSNorm, argmax), softmax, EinSum, ReLU, MaxPool, range checks, lookup tables, and commitment schemes — live in [`docs/`](docs/) and are built with [mdBook](https://github.com/rust-lang/mdBook) + [mdbook-katex](https://github.com/lzanini/mdbook-katex):\nL30: \nL31: ```bash\nL32: cargo install mdbook\nL33: cargo install mdbook-katex\nL34: mdbook build docs --open\nL35: ```\nL36: ## Supported Models & Layers\nL37: ### Transformer (LLM)\nL38: \nL39: The recommended path is **SafeTensors via Hugging Face**: pass `--hf <model-id>` and `bench-llm` will fetch and cache the weights automatically on first run. GGUF (`--gguf <path|url>`) and ONNX models are also supported.\nL40: | Model | Recommended loader | Notes |\nL41: |-------|--------------------|-------|\nL42: | **GPT-2** | `--hf openai-community/gpt2` | SafeTensors auto-downloaded from HF. GGUF also supported (`--gguf gpt2.Q2_K.gguf`) |\nL43: | **Gemma 3** | `--hf google/gemma-3-1b-it` | SafeTensors only |\nL44: | **Llama 2** | `--hf meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf` | SafeTensors only. `--accuracy` mode currently falls back to default quantization for Llama 2 |\nL45: \nL46: Supported transformer layers:\nL47: - **Embeddings** — provable token vocabulary lookup\nL48: - **Positional encoding** — Absolute and Rotary (RoPE)\nL49: - **Attention mask** — causal masking for auto-regressive generation\nL50: - **EinSum / Multi-head attention** — batched matrix multiplication for QK^T and OV projections\nL51: - **Softmax** — with provable range checks\nL52: - **LayerNorm / RMSNorm** — vector normalization with proofs\nL53: - **Argmax / Logits** — next-token prediction output layer\nL54: ### Traditional (MLP / CNN)\nL55: \nL56: - **Dense** — fully connected layers\nL57: - **Conv2D** — convolutions with FFT-based optimization\nL58: - **Activations** — ReLU, GELU\nL59: - **Pooling** — MaxPool\nL60: - **Reshape / Flatten / Add** — tensor manipulation and residual connections\nL61: \nL62: ## Installation\nL63: \nL64: ### Prerequisites\nL65: \nL66: - **Rust + Cargo** (latest stable)\nL67: - **Python 3.10+** — only required if you plan to run the MLP/CNN benchmark harness `bench.py`\nL68: - **CUDA 12.x toolkit + NVIDIA driver** — only required for the GPU build\nL69: ### Build (CPU)\nL70: \nL71: ```bash\nL72: curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh\nL73: source $HOME/.cargo/env\nL74: cargo build --release -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL75: ```\nL76: ### Build (GPU)\nL77: \nL78: DeepProve ships two GPU feature flags:\nL79: \nL80: | Feature | Platform | Effect |\nL81: |---------|----------|--------|\nL82: | `cuda` | Linux + NVIDIA | Replaces the CPU PCS with `HyperKZGGpu` and runs inference on burn's CUDA backend — **fastest path** |\nL83: | `wgpu` | Linux / macOS / Windows | Cross-platform GPU inference; PCS still runs on CPU |\nL84: \nL85: ```bash\nL86: # Linux + NVIDIA: full GPU acceleration (inference + PCS)\nL87: cargo build --release --features cuda -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL88: # Cross-platform GPU inference only\nL89: cargo build --release --features wgpu -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL90: ```\nL91: \nL92: The default (no `--features` flag) is a CPU-only build using the `ndarray` backend.\nL93: \nL94: ## Quickstart: Prove GPT-2 Inference End-to-End\nL95: \nL96: This walkthrough builds the binary, fetches GPT-2 from Hugging Face, and produces an end-to-end inference proof.\nL97: \nL98: ### 1. Build\nL99: \nL100: ```bash\nL101: cargo build --release -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL102: ```\nL103: ### 2. Get a GPT-2 model\nL104: \nL105: The recommended path is `--hf openai-community/gpt2`: `bench-llm` will fetch the SafeTensors weights and tokenizer from Hugging Face on first run and cache them locally for subsequent runs — no manual download required. SafeTensors is the most widely-used checkpoint format for transformer models, which is why we lead with it.\nL106: GGUF (`--gguf <path|url>`) and ONNX checkpoints are also supported; the GGUF path is convenient if you already have a pre-quantized file (e.g. `gpt2.Q2_K.gguf`).\nL107: ### 3. Prove a single sequence\nL108: \nL109: ```bash\nL110: cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL111:   --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64\nL112: ```\nL113: \nL114: This builds the proving context, runs inference on a 64-token random prompt, generates a proof, and verifies it. Two CSV files are produced (see *Reading the output* below).\nL115: ### 4. Sweep multiple sequence lengths\nL116: \nL117: ```bash\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/238.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/238.pdf: (403) Forbidden"
